<section class="band">
  <h2 class="section-title">Your questions, answered</h2>
  <div class="faq">
    <details>
      <summary>Who sees me?</summary>
      <p>Aircraft fitted with ADS-L. skyBlip Go also receives FLARM, but transmits only ADS-L.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Do I see airliners?</summary>
      <p>
        Not with a Go alone. Airliners transmit ADS-B on 1090&nbsp;MHz, which a
        Go does not receive. skyBlip Bravo receives it. Use it with your
        phone or pair it with your Go as a Tango:
        <%= link_to_page "skyblip-bravo", label: "skyBlip Bravo" %>.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Does Bravo see every aircraft with a transponder?</summary>
      <p>
        No. Bravo receives ADS-B. A Mode S transponder sends it only if
        it has the ADS-B out function, also called Extended Squitter. Without
        that function, it is invisible to Bravo and any other ADS-B receiver.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Does Bravo make me visible?</summary>
      <p>
        No. Bravo only receives traffic. A Go broadcasts your position in ADS-L,
        on its own or paired with a Bravo as a Tango.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>I fly a glider with FLARM. What does skyBlip add?</summary>
      <p>
        A Go broadcasts your position in ADS-L, for the aircraft that receive
        ADS-L. It does not transmit FLARM and does not replace your FLARM unit.
        If your FLARM unit already shows ADS-B traffic, check what a Bravo
        would add before you buy one.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Is there a subscription?</summary>
      <p>No. Once you own a Go, everything works: transmitting, receiving, the alarm, updates. The firmware is free, including Bravo&rsquo;s.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Is it certified?</summary>
      <p>
        No. It helps you look out for traffic. It does not replace looking outside or the
        transponder where one is required.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Do I need a phone?</summary>
      <p>
        Not with a Go. The radar, the alarm and the settings are on the device.
        The phone is for seeing the traffic in your navigation app. With a Bravo
        on its own, you need a phone to see the traffic, since Bravo has no screen.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Is there another version?</summary>
      <p>
        Yes. skyBlip Go+ runs on a LilyGO T-Echo Plus, with a buzzer and
        vibration for alarms and a bigger battery.
        <%= link_to_page "buy", label: "Compare", anchor: "compare" %>.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>What if I want to go back?</summary>
      <p>
        The skyBlip firmware leaves the T-Echo&rsquo;s factory bootloader
        (its startup program) unchanged. You can reinstall the other software available for it
        as before.
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band band--tint">
  <h2 class="section-title">ADS-L, <span class="highlight">the common language</span> of everything that flies</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">
    ADS-L is the European standard for sharing positions by radio, published by EASA. It
    covers everything from light aircraft to skydivers, by way of
    microlights, gliders, gyroplanes, helicopters, balloons and paragliders,
    and tomorrow drones.
  </p>
  <div class="columns">
    <div>
      <h3>Open.</h3>
      <p>Anyone can read and use the public specification. skyBlip&rsquo;s code for it is open source.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Lightweight.</h3>
      <p>A message lasting a few milliseconds is sent once a second on the licence-free 868&nbsp;MHz band. A battery-powered pocket device is enough.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Interoperable.</h3>
      <p>Its channels follow those of FLARM and PilotAware. skyBlip Go also receives FLARM traffic.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

